#> tool: sedrepl
#> wait: one-liners for sed
#> pause: 50
#> must: one-liners for sed
#> must: # command:  sed  -f
#> not: Subject: one
#> not: From:
#> not: Hello
#> source: s///;q
# 26. Pull one header out of a mail message. Pement: sed '/^Subject: */!d; s///;q'
# Every line that does not begin with the header name is deleted. On the one
# that does, s/// reuses the last pattern (an empty regex means "the regex
# used most recently") and removes it; q then stops. The sheet also has the
# whole header,  sed '/^$/q' , and the body,  sed '1,/^$/d'  (everything
# up to the first blank line goes).
# Try: 1,/^$/d  to see only the body.
/^Subject: */!d; s///;q

#DATA
From: Eric Pement <pemente@example.org>
To: sed-users
Subject: one-liners for sed

Hello all,
the new version is up.
